.TH LETHEFS 4
.SH NAME
lethefs, lethed \- stateless read-only file service (Lethe)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lethefs
.B -A
[
.B -D
]
.I addr
.I mtpt
.PP
.B lethed
.B -A
[
.B -v
] [
.B -a
.I addr
]
.I root
.SH DESCRIPTION
Lethe is a file protocol for static trees in which the server keeps no state
between requests: every request names its file by an absolute path, so a
server can restart, or a client move to another replica, in the middle of a
read.
It is the protocol
.B lethe1
of the Purgatorio systems, with which these programs interoperate.
.PP
.I Lethefs
dials the server at
.I addr
(default port 5640) and mounts its tree at
.IR mtpt ,
by serving Styx on a pipe; the fids Styx needs are kept by
.I lethefs
itself.
The tree is read-only.
.B -D
prints the Styx messages.
.PP
.I Lethed
serves the tree at
.IR root ,
a name in its own name space, on
.I addr
(default
.BR tcp!*!5640 ).
Requests are answered as the user
.BR none :
a file must be readable, and each directory above it searchable, by others.
.PP
Only Lethe's clear mode is implemented, which has no authentication and no
encryption, and
.B -A
must be given to say so.
Neither program falls back to it otherwise.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/lethefs.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/lethed.b
.SH BUGS
No tickets, no sealing, and no caching in
.IR lethefs :
each Styx operation is one round trip or more.
